#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""xray-omarchy: one xray core for the tunnel (subscriptions, config, TUN, killswitch, status).

The xray core holds the TUN device (xray-omarchy0), the routing (local and
direct zones past the tunnel), DNS on 127.0.0.1:53 and the node choice. This
program builds the config, sets the routes, the killswitch (nftables table
inet xray-omarchy) and the system DNS through the root helper
/usr/lib/xray-omarchy/xray-omarchy-helper (sudo without a password only for
it), and gives the bar plugin the whole state as one JSON.

Nothing about the machine is hard-coded: the physical interface, the
NetworkManager connection and the network's DNS are detected
(~/.local/state/xray-omarchy/network.json); the rest are optional settings in
~/.config/xray-omarchy/settings.json (lan_domains, direct_geo, local_nets, lan_dns).

The xray core is the official XTLS release. `core update` downloads it as the
user; the root helper copies it into /var/lib/xray-omarchy/core, installs it only
when its SHA-256 is one this package pins (/usr/share/xray-omarchy/core-sums.json)
and gives it CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE. geoip.dat and geosite.dat
come from the distribution (/usr/share/v2ray, packages v2ray-geoip and
v2ray-domain-list-community).

Secrets: subscriptions, their cache and the built config are 0600 files.
The connection log (where sites went) lives only in tmpfs
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xray-omarchy and disappears when the core stops.

XRAY_OMARCHY_* variables (directories, unit, API ports, xray path) are for a
test stand only: with them the commands work on a copy and leave the live core alone.
"""
